from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "12. Where the Healing Gift Ends"),
("p", "Claim: the last healing the Scriptures record is at Malta, in Acts 28:8, 9, and the chapter that records it ends with Israel’s program nationally set aside. The volume holds that the gifts of healing, as a sign-gift, ended with the sign-program they served. No verse states this. It is an inference from the program, and the volume says so. Volume 1 argues the end of the sign-program, Volume 2 applies it to tongues and prophecy, and Acts Commentary Volume 5 argues the suspension itself; this chapter applies it to healing."),
("sub", "12.1 The last recorded healing"),
("p", "“And it came to pass, that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever and of a bloody flux: to whom Paul entered in, and prayed, and laid his hands on him, and healed him. So when this was done, others also, which had diseases in the island, came, and were healed: Who also honoured us with many honours; and when we departed, they laded us with such things as were necessary” (Acts 28:8–10). The narrator records a healing and a crowd healed. After Malta the book records no further healing. Paul reaches Rome, and the book ends with his two years there: “Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him, Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him” (Acts 28:30, 31). No healing is recorded in those two years."),
("sub", "12.2 What follows at Rome"),
("p", "Before those verses Paul speaks to the leaders of the Jews. “Well spake the Holy Ghost by Esaias the prophet unto our fathers, Saying, Go unto this people, and say, Hearing ye shall hear, and shall not understand; and seeing ye shall see, and not perceive” (Acts 28:25, 26). The quotation ends in the words, “lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and should be converted, and I should heal them” (Acts 28:27). Paul closes with the verdict: “Be it known therefore unto you, that the salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles, and that they will hear it” (Acts 28:28). The last word of Isaiah’s quotation is “heal.” The LORD says that he would heal this people if they turned, and the book records that they did not."),
("p", "The volume does not say that the word “heal” in Isaiah 6:10 is a statement about the gifts of healing. It reports that the passage which marks the close of Israel’s hearing in the book ends on the word “heal,” and that the book’s last recorded healing stands a few verses before it."),
("sub", "12.3 The inference"),
("p", "The healings of the Lord and of the Twelve were signs to Israel (chapters 4 and 5), and Paul’s own healings were done in settings shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, which remained operative throughout Acts 9–28 even as the Mystery Program began. Acts 9–28 is the overlap; where Paul or his companions are the ones working signs, the volume reports the text and does not assign the event to a single program. With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically. Signs do not continue into the Mystery Program."),
("p", "The inference to the gifts of healing follows. The gifts were given to the Corinthians in the overlap (chapter 7), and they served the sign-program of that period. When the program ended, the gift that served it ended with it. Healing ends programmatically at Acts 28. No verse states it; the program ends, and the gift ends with it. The volume does not say that the Corinthian gifts stopped on a given day. It says that the Scriptures record no gift of healing after the program’s close, that the letters to the Body written after that point name none, and that the Body’s pattern in them is grace sufficient."),
("sub", "12.4 What the ruling does not deny"),
("p", "The cessation of the prophetic sign-gifts at Acts 28 does not deny God’s sovereign ability to heal, nor does it deny that believers may pray for the sick. What ceased is the programmatic, public, sign-authentication authority given in the Kingdom ministry and continued to Israel through Acts 1–28. In the Body of Christ today, healing is never a sign-gift, never an apostolic authority and never a guaranteed outcome. It is always God’s sovereign mercy, never a continuation of the Kingdom signs. This chapter makes no statement about God’s power; it governs the programs."),
("p", "The ground for praying is the ground of chapter 9: Paul commands prayer in everything. A believer who prays for a sick friend does what Philippians 4:6 says. A recovery that follows is the mercy of God, as it was for Epaphroditus. The volume does not judge any report of recovery in any assembly, and it does not say that no one is ever healed. It says that no one in the Body holds a gift or an authority that makes healing a sign."),
("sub", "12.5 The letter to the Hebrews"),
("p", "The letter to the Hebrews names the sign-gifts of the first generation. “How shall we escape, if we neglect so great salvation; which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord, and was confirmed unto us by them that heard him; God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will?” (Hebrews 2:3, 4). The verse is written to Hebrew readers within Israel’s prophetic setting, and this reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting. It records that signs confirmed the message to them “at the first,” and it says nothing of the Body."),
("tbl", "The end of the healing gift.", [
    ("Acts 28:8", T("Acts 28:8"), "The last recorded healing in the book: the father of Publius."),
    ("Acts 28:27", T("Acts 28:27"), "Isaiah’s quotation closes on “heal” and on a people not converted."),
    ("Acts 28:28", T("Acts 28:28"), "The salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles."),
    ("Acts 28:31", T("Acts 28:31"), "Two years at Rome, preaching and teaching; no healing recorded."),
    ("Hebrews 2:4", T("Heb 2:4"), "God bore witness with signs and wonders at the first."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Acts 28:8, 9 is the last recorded healing, and the book records none afterward.",
    "The quotation of Isaiah at Rome ends on “heal,” and the book closes on Israel’s hearing set aside.",
    "The end of the gift of healing is an inference from the end of the sign-program, and no verse states it.",
    "The ruling makes no statement about God’s power and does not deny prayer for the sick.",
  ]),
]
